problem:
When a Slack app starts a new session, it has to load a lot before anything works: your channel list, your preferences, your recent conversations. That loading leans on two layers:

- underneath, the database (Slack calls its database layer Vitess);
- in front of it, a large cache that holds copies of recently used data, so most requests never reach the database at all.

Two pieces manage that cache. Mcrouter spreads requests across the many cache servers, and a newer piece called Mcrib decides which cache servers are in use at any moment. Mcrib does this by watching Consul, a system that keeps a live list of which machines are up. When a cache server drops off that list, Mcrib swaps in an empty spare. If a server later rejoins, Mcrib empties it first, so it can never hand back data that went stale while it was away.

{{figure:cache-routing-layer}}

Its designers knew empty cache servers are risky, so the goal was to keep the set of servers stable and wipe one only when necessary, and Mcrib was genuinely better at this than the older system it replaced.

The week's maintenance was an upgrade to Consul itself, rolled out to 25% of the machines at a time, with the restarts kept slow and one at a time to avoid disturbing anything. While a machine restarts, it drops off Consul's live list and then rejoins. So from Mcrib's point of view, cache servers kept briefly 'dying,' and Mcrib kept dutifully replacing them with empty ones. Two of these 25% rounds had finished the prior week without incident. The third ran on February 22 and crossed a tipping point as the day hit peak traffic: enough of the cache was empty at exactly the moment demand was highest.

The danger was hidden in how one kind of data was stored. When the app starts, it asks which people are in your group direct messages, and that membership data was stored in a table split up by user. Splitting by user is efficient for finding a user's channels, but it is the wrong way around for finding a channel's members. To answer the backwards question, the database has to check every split (each split is called a 'shard') at once. Normally that was invisible, because the answer never changes and so stays in the cache for a long time, almost never reaching the database. But with the cache partly empty, nearly every starting app forced a query against every shard, and database load climbed far faster than the share of requests that missed the cache, until more and more of those queries timed out. Those timeouts were what sealed the loop: a query that times out never fills the cache, and an empty cache guarantees the next miss.

{{figure:the-scatter-amplifier}}

solution:
The response ran on two tracks: understand the load, and reduce it. The reduction is a study in shedding load under pressure. The team specifically limited how many apps could start up, and they knew exactly what that meant. Users not yet connected would stay out, so users already connected kept working, and the lighter query load would let the cache start filling. The first, restrictive limit worked. Easing that limit for the first time taught the other hard lesson of this class. They raised the limit too far, database load shot past what it could handle again, failures climbed, and they had to drop back down and climb in smaller steps. Near a tipping point, the safe way back to full traffic is small steps, watching the useful work getting through at every one.

{{figure:shed-and-recover}}

The trap itself was fixed in place. The expensive query was changed to fetch from the database only the entries actually missing from the cache, instead of re-fetching everything on any miss. And because this membership data never changes, so a slightly old copy is fine, the query was also allowed to read from the database's read-only copies, not just the main copy, which multiplied the capacity available to serve it. With the trap weakened and traffic held below the tipping point, the loop ran in reverse: the cache filled, hit rates rose, and database load fell. The cap on new startups was then raised back to normal in steps, keeping useful work high, until service was fully restored.

The lessons are the best part of the write-up. The upgrade had been paused early, and it changed nothing. Slack looks closely at Mcrib, and does not pin the outage on it. It contributed 'not by being incorrect or inefficient, quite the opposite': it detected and replaced dropped cache servers faster than the old system ever could, and that very speed amplified the churn. In Slack's words, it is 'objectively a better system, but its efficiency made the broader system behave in a less safe way.' The expensive query was permanently moved to a table split up by channel instead, and the rest of the queries that sit behind a cache were audited for the same shape of risk. Slack also noted that a brief network outage hitting the cache servers could set off the same cascade, and is changing how Mcrib works to guard against it. Nolan closes with Richard Cook's 'How Complex Systems Fail': complex systems always carry latent flaws, each harmless on its own, waiting for the combination. And Slack publishes these, she writes, so the rest of the industry can learn from their worst Tuesday.

